---
// What makes a good prompt, and a bad one: seven marks with a weak and a strong example each,
// the cultural iceberg, and where the bank's prompts come from. The marks are data in
// src/content/craft.ts; the strong examples are quoted from the bank as it reads today. Every
// example is shown on its card, drawn at build time by the same code as the tool.
import { faAnglesDown, faAnglesUp } from '@fortawesome/free-solid-svg-icons';
import { article, ref, wikipedia } from '@eurth/style/build';
import Fa from '@eurth/style/components/Fa.astro';
import Figure from '@eurth/style/components/Figure.astro';
import Line from '@eurth/style/components/Line.astro';
import Note from '@eurth/style/components/Note.astro';
import Page from '@eurth/style/components/Page.astro';
import PageHead from '@eurth/style/components/PageHead.astro';
import Toc from '@eurth/style/components/Toc.astro';
import { lineArrowRight } from '@eurth/style/line';
import { site } from '../../eurth.config.mjs';
import mark from '../assets/mark.svg?raw';
import { ICEBERG, MARKS, UPDATED, WEAK_LABEL } from '../content/craft';
import { BANK } from '../prompts/bank';
import { altText, label } from '../prompts/describe';
import { promptPath } from '../prompts/link';
import { find } from '../prompts/model';
import { cardPicture } from '../site/picture';

const title = 'What makes a good worldbuilding prompt';
const description = 'Seven marks of a good worldbuilding prompt, each with a weak example and a strong one, the cultural iceberg model, and where the prompts in this bank come from.';
const fromForum = BANK.filter((entry) => entry.from === 'forum').length;
const out = (href: string) => ref(href, site.host);
const head = find(BANK, MARKS[0]!.strong)!;
const sections = [
  { id: 'marks', label: 'Seven marks of a good prompt' },
  { id: 'iceberg', label: 'The iceberg under every culture' },
  { id: 'forum', label: 'Where these prompts come from' },
  { id: 'own', label: 'Write your own' },
];
---

<Page site={site} title={title} description={description} crumbs={[{ name: 'Good prompts', path: '/good-prompts/' }]} jsonld={[article(site, { headline: title, path: '/good-prompts/', description, updated: UPDATED })]}>
  <Fragment slot="mark" set:html={mark} />
  <PageHead
    site={site}
    kicker="Good prompts"
    title={title}
    updated={UPDATED}
    lead="A good prompt makes you decide something you hadn't thought to decide. A bad one asks for a list. Here is the difference, in seven marks, with an example of each going wrong and going right."
  >
    <a slot="art" href={promptPath(head.n)}><img src={cardPicture(head, site)} alt={altText(head, site.host)} width="1200" height="630" /></a>
  </PageHead>

  <div class="eu-article">
    <article class="eu-prose">
      <h2 id="marks">Seven marks of a good prompt</h2>
      <p>Every prompt in the <a href="/archive/">archive</a> was held against these. None of the weak examples is in the bank; all of the strong ones are.</p>
      <Note>Each strong example opens on its own page, where you can answer it.</Note>

      {
        MARKS.map((mark, index) => {
          const strong = find(BANK, mark.strong)!;
          return (
            <>
              <h3 id={mark.id}>
                {index + 1}. {mark.title}
              </h3>
              <p>{mark.why}</p>
              <div class="eu-figures">
                <Figure verdict="bad" verdictLabel="Weak." caption={mark.trouble}>
                  <img
                    src={cardPicture({ n: 0, theme: strong.theme, text: mark.weak }, site, { label: WEAK_LABEL })}
                    alt={`A weak prompt: "${mark.weak}"`}
                    width="1200"
                    height="630"
                    loading="lazy"
                  />
                </Figure>
                <Figure verdict="good" verdictLabel="Strong.">
                  <a href={promptPath(strong.n)}>
                    <img src={cardPicture(strong, site)} alt={`A strong prompt: "${strong.text}"`} width="1200" height="630" loading="lazy" />
                  </a>
                  <Fragment slot="caption">
                    {mark.gain} ({label(strong)})
                  </Fragment>
                </Figure>
              </div>
            </>
          );
        })
      }

      <h2 id="iceberg">The iceberg under every culture</h2>
      <p>
        In 1976 the anthropologist <a href={wikipedia('Edward T. Hall', site.host)}>Edward T. Hall</a> compared a culture to an iceberg, in his book{' '}
        <a href={wikipedia('Beyond Culture', site.host)}><cite>Beyond Culture</cite></a>. The part a visitor sees in a week is the tip. Most of what makes a people who
        they are lies under the water, where it takes years to learn and nobody thinks to explain it.
      </p>

      <div class="eu-figures">
        <section class="eu-card" aria-labelledby="above">
          <h3 class="eu-card-title" id="above"><Fa icon={faAnglesUp} /> Above the water</h3>
          <p class="eu-help">What a visitor sees in a week.</p>
          <ul>
            {ICEBERG.above.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="eu-card eu-card--outline" aria-labelledby="below">
          <h3 class="eu-card-title" id="below"><Fa icon={faAnglesDown} /> Below the water</h3>
          <p class="eu-help">What takes years to learn.</p>
          <ul>
            {ICEBERG.below.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </section>
      </div>

      <p>
        Worldbuilding tends to stop at the tip, because the tip is easy to list: a national dish, a national dress, a flag. A nation built that way reads like a
        tourist brochure. The fix is to start at the tip, where it is easy, and ask a question whose answer is under the water. Who cooks the national dish, and who
        only eats it? Who may wear the dress, and who is laughed at for trying?
      </p>
      <p>
        Orioni wrote the model up for worldbuilders on the Eurth forum, with the diagram:{' '}
        <a href={out('https://eurth.org/t/cultural-iceberg-model/17682')}>Cultural Iceberg Model</a>. Worldbuilding Magazine's podcast has an episode on the same
        idea, <a href={out('https://anchor.fm/worldcasting0/episodes/The-Iceberg-Principle-edli20')}>The Iceberg Principle</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="forum">Where these prompts come from</h2>
      <p>
        Eurth's members have been asking each other awkward questions about their nations for years.
        {fromForum} of the {BANK.length} prompts here began on the forum. The rest were written for this site in the same spirit.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href={out('https://eurth.org/t/worldbuilding-prompts/17685')}>Worldbuilding Prompts</a>, the forum's own list: some two hundred questions on geography,
          history, politics, the economy, demographics and culture, and a last section on tying them together.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={out('https://eurth.org/t/december-2020-event-daily-worldbuilding-prompts/3695')}>December 2020: Daily Worldbuilding Prompts</a>, when the staff
          posted a prompt a day for a month and members answered in the thread. Weekends, pets, funerals and gambling all had their day. The answers are worth
          reading as examples.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The forum's prompts are longer than a card allows: many ask four questions at once, which suits a factbook written over an afternoon. Here each is cut to one
        or two sentences, for ten minutes on one day. If a prompt leaves you wanting the rest, the full list is where to go next.
      </p>

      <h2 id="own">Write your own</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Pick something a visitor to your nation could see or touch: a bridge, a coin, a uniform, a queue.</li>
        <li>State one thing about it as already true. Make it slightly inconvenient.</li>
        <li>Ask one question about people: who gains, who pays, who objects, who remembers.</li>
        <li>Cut it to two sentences, and check that a nation quite unlike yours could still answer it.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Then try it on someone. A prompt is good if two people give answers that could not be swapped.</p>
    </article>
    <Toc items={sections} />
  </div>

  <div class="eu-next">
    <div class="eu-next-words">
      <h2>Answer one</h2>
      <p>The <a href="/guide/">guide</a> shows how to answer a prompt in ten minutes, alone or with a group. Today's is waiting.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="eu-btn eu-btn--primary" href="/">Today's prompt <Line icon={lineArrowRight} /></a>
  </div>
</Page>
